  var BUS2_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'Why is good communication important to a business?', options: ['It makes sure everyone understands instructions and decisions correctly', 'It guarantees that a business will always make a profit every year', 'It removes the need for any management structure in the business', 'It is only important for businesses that sell goods internationally'], correct: 0, explain: 'Good communication ensures information, instructions and decisions are passed on accurately, helping a business run efficiently and avoid costly mistakes.', misconception: 'Communication is not a guarantee of profit — it is one factor that supports efficient, effective operations.', tag: 'Communication' },
    { q: 'What problem is most likely to result from ineffective communication in a business?', options: ['Misunderstandings, mistakes and delays', 'Automatically higher wages for all staff', 'A guaranteed increase in sales revenue', 'A reduced need for training of new staff'], correct: 0, explain: 'Ineffective communication can lead to misunderstandings, errors and delays, which can reduce efficiency and damage relationships with staff and customers.', misconception: 'Poor communication does not affect wages or sales directly — its main impact is on accuracy, efficiency and understanding.', tag: 'Communication' },
    { q: 'Which of these is an example of a method of communication a business might use?', options: ['Email, meetings, memos or video calls', 'Recruitment of new staff', 'Training of new employees', 'Delegation of tasks to staff in a team'], correct: 0, explain: 'Businesses use a range of communication methods, including email, meetings, memos, phone calls and video conferencing, to share information.', misconception: 'Recruitment, training and delegation are separate business activities, not methods of communication.', tag: 'Communication' },
    { q: 'What is a key benefit of using information technology (IT) for business communication?', options: ['It allows fast, often instant communication over long distances', 'It removes the need for any communication barriers to be considered', 'It always costs less than face-to-face communication', 'It guarantees the message will be understood correctly'], correct: 0, explain: 'IT, such as email and video calls, allows businesses to communicate quickly and over long distances, supporting efficient decision-making.', misconception: 'IT communication can still suffer from barriers, such as technical problems or unclear messages — speed does not guarantee understanding.', tag: 'Communication' },
    { q: 'Which of these is a barrier to effective communication?', options: ['Jargon, noise or an unsuitable method', 'Choosing an appropriate method of communication', 'Removing barriers to communication at work', 'Having a clear chain of command in the business'], correct: 0, explain: 'Barriers to communication include using jargon, background noise, a poor choice of method, or language differences, which can all prevent a message being understood.', misconception: 'A poor choice of method is itself a barrier — choosing the right method actually helps reduce barriers, not create them.', tag: 'Communication' },
    { q: 'What is the purpose of a job description?', options: ['To set out the duties and responsibilities of a specific job', 'To list a candidate’s qualifications and full work history', 'To record an employee’s attendance and holiday each year', 'To set out the business’s financial objectives for the year'], correct: 0, explain: 'A job description sets out the duties, responsibilities and purpose of a specific role, helping both the employer and applicants understand what the job involves.', misconception: 'A job description describes the role itself; a CV or application form is where a candidate’s own qualifications and history are recorded.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'What does a person specification outline?', options: ['The skills and qualities needed in the ideal candidate', 'The financial objectives of the business for the year', 'The duties and responsibilities of a particular job role', 'The wage the business is willing to pay for the role'], correct: 0, explain: 'A person specification lists the qualifications, skills, experience and personal qualities an employer is looking for in the ideal candidate.', misconception: 'This is different from a job description, which focuses on the role’s duties rather than the candidate’s qualities.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of internal recruitment?', options: ['It is quicker and cheaper, and the candidate is already known', 'It always brings in fresh ideas and skills from outside the business', 'It guarantees a much wider range of candidates to choose from', 'It removes the need for any training once the person is appointed'], correct: 0, explain: 'Internal recruitment can be faster and less costly, and the business already has knowledge of the employee’s performance and abilities.', misconception: 'Bringing in fresh ideas and a wider pool of candidates is more typical of external recruitment, not internal.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of external recruitment?', options: ['It brings in new skills, experience and ideas', 'It is always faster than recruiting from inside the business', 'It never requires any induction training for the new employee', 'It guarantees lower recruitment costs than internal recruitment'], correct: 0, explain: 'External recruitment allows a business to access a wider pool of candidates and bring in new skills, perspectives and experience not already present internally.', misconception: 'External recruitment is often slower and more costly than internal recruitment, and new external hires typically still need induction training.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'What is the purpose of shortlisting in the recruitment process?', options: ['To narrow applicants down to those who best match the role', 'To advertise the job vacancy as widely as possible', 'To train new employees once they have been appointed', 'To identify and dismiss underperforming members of staff'], correct: 0, explain: 'Shortlisting involves selecting the most suitable applicants from all those who applied, based on how well they match the job description and person specification.', misconception: 'Shortlisting happens after advertising and applications, and before interviews — it is a distinct stage in the recruitment process.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'What is the main purpose of interviewing candidates?', options: ['To assess a candidate’s suitability through questions and discussion', 'To advertise the vacancy to a wider audience of possible applicants', 'To draw up the job description and the person specification', 'To calculate the wages and benefits to be offered for the role'], correct: 0, explain: 'Interviews allow an employer to assess a candidate’s skills, experience, personality and suitability for a role through direct conversation.', misconception: 'Interviews come after shortlisting, not before advertising or drawing up the job description.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'How does equal opportunities legislation affect recruitment?', options: ['It bans discrimination based on gender, race, disability, religion or age', 'It requires every business to hire an equal number of men and women', 'It only applies to public sector employers such as schools and hospitals', 'It prevents businesses from ever rejecting a candidate for a job'], correct: 0, explain: 'Equal opportunities legislation makes it illegal to discriminate in recruitment or employment based on protected characteristics such as gender, race, disability, religion, sexual preference and age.', misconception: 'The law protects against discrimination — it does not require an exact balance of characteristics among staff, and applies to private and public sector employers alike.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'How does a national minimum wage law affect businesses?', options: ['It sets a legal minimum hourly rate of pay', 'It sets the maximum amount any employee can be paid', 'It only applies to full-time employees over 25', 'It has no effect on a business’s wage costs'], correct: 0, explain: 'A minimum wage law sets the lowest hourly rate an employer can legally pay, which affects a business’s wage costs and pricing decisions.', misconception: 'Minimum wage laws set a floor, not a ceiling, on pay, and typically apply across different types of employment, not just full-time roles.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'Which type of employment involves a worker being contracted for a set number of hours each week on an ongoing basis?', options: ['Full-time employment', 'Casual employment', 'Seasonal employment', 'Temporary employment'], correct: 0, explain: 'Full-time employment typically involves a worker being contracted for a standard, ongoing number of hours each week, such as 35–40 hours.', misconception: 'Casual, seasonal and temporary employment are usually short-term or irregular, unlike the ongoing, set-hours nature of full-time work.', tag: 'Recruitment' },
    { q: 'Why is training important to a business?', options: ['It improves staff skills, productivity and quality of work', 'It guarantees that no employee will ever leave the business', 'It replaces the need for any recruitment of new staff', 'It is only relevant to new employees in their first week'], correct: 0, explain: 'Training helps employees develop the skills and knowledge needed to do their job well, which can improve productivity, quality and staff motivation.', misconception: 'Training does not replace recruitment or guarantee staff retention — it is one factor that can support both.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'What is the main purpose of induction training?', options: ['To help a new employee settle in and learn the business’s policies', 'To teach experienced employees new and more advanced skills', 'To train staff off-site so that they gain a recognised qualification', 'To assess an employee’s performance before deciding on a promotion'], correct: 0, explain: 'Induction training introduces new employees to the business, its policies, health and safety procedures and their specific role.', misconception: 'Induction is aimed at new starters settling in, not experienced staff learning advanced skills.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of on-the-job training?', options: ['Staff learn while still producing work, often at lower cost', 'It always provides a nationally recognised qualification for the trainee', 'It removes any risk of mistakes affecting the business’s production', 'It requires no input or supervision from experienced members of staff'], correct: 0, explain: 'On-the-job training takes place at the workplace, allowing employees to keep working and producing output while they learn, usually at a lower cost.', misconception: 'On-the-job training can still lead to mistakes affecting production and usually relies on experienced staff or supervisors to guide the trainee.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of off-the-job training?', options: ['Staff learn away from work pressures, often gaining qualifications', 'It is always cheaper than training employees on the job', 'It means employees keep producing output while they are training', 'It never disrupts the trainee’s normal duties or the team’s workload'], correct: 0, explain: 'Off-the-job training takes place away from the workplace, such as at a college, allowing focused learning and often leading to formal qualifications.', misconception: 'Off-the-job training tends to be more expensive, and because it happens away from the workplace, the employee is not producing output during that time.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'How can training help a business comply with health and safety laws?', options: ['It ensures staff understand safe practices and the law, reducing accidents', 'It removes the need for the business to follow health and safety law', 'It only relates to employees who work in offices, not in factories or shops', 'It has no connection to legal compliance or to the cost of accidents'], correct: 0, explain: 'Training employees on safe working practices helps a business meet its legal health and safety obligations and reduces the risk of accidents at work.', misconception: 'Training supports legal compliance — it does not remove the underlying legal requirement to provide a safe working environment.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'Which best describes the difference between on-the-job and off-the-job training?', options: ['On-the-job is at work; off-the-job is away from the workplace', 'On-the-job training always costs much more than off-the-job training', 'Off-the-job training never leads to a qualification for the employee', 'There is no meaningful difference between the two types of training'], correct: 0, explain: 'On-the-job training takes place at the employee’s normal workplace while they continue working, whereas off-the-job training takes place elsewhere, such as a college or training centre.', misconception: 'Off-the-job training is often more likely, not less likely, to lead to a formal, recognised qualification.', tag: 'Training' },
    { q: 'Why is motivation important in the workplace?', options: ['Motivated staff tend to work harder, boosting productivity', 'Motivation guarantees that a business will always make a profit', 'Motivation removes the need for any training of new employees', 'Motivation is only relevant to senior managers, not other staff'], correct: 0, explain: 'Motivated employees are generally more productive, engaged and willing to put in effort, which can benefit the whole business.', misconception: 'Motivation supports performance but does not on its own guarantee profit, and it matters for staff at all levels, not just managers.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'According to Maslow’s theory of motivation, what must be satisfied before higher-level needs can motivate a person?', options: ['Basic needs, such as physiological and safety needs', 'Only self-actualisation needs at the top of the hierarchy', 'Esteem needs, such as recognition, on their own', 'No order: all needs matter equally and at the same time'], correct: 0, explain: 'Maslow’s hierarchy suggests that lower-level needs, such as physiological and safety needs, must generally be met before higher-level needs like esteem or self-actualisation can motivate someone.', misconception: 'Maslow’s theory proposes an order of needs, not that all needs motivate equally regardless of whether basic needs are met.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'What did Herzberg identify as separate from motivators in his two-factor theory?', options: ['Hygiene factors, such as pay and conditions', 'Only financial rewards such as bonuses and commission', 'Only training and promotion opportunities at work', 'Physical needs, such as food, water and shelter'], correct: 0, explain: 'Herzberg’s two-factor theory distinguishes hygiene factors, such as pay, working conditions and job security, from motivators, such as recognition and responsibility.', misconception: 'Physical needs like food and water belong to Maslow’s theory, not Herzberg’s hygiene and motivator factors.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'What was a key feature of Taylor’s theory of motivation?', options: ['Workers are motivated by money and should be paid by output', 'Workers are motivated mainly by self-actualisation and personal growth', 'Money has no effect at all on how motivated workers feel at work', 'Workers should be given complete freedom over how they do their tasks'], correct: 0, explain: 'Taylor believed workers were mainly motivated by financial reward, and advocated paying employees based on the amount they produced (piece-rate pay).', misconception: 'Taylor’s view focused on financial motivation and output-based pay, unlike Maslow or Herzberg, who emphasised a wider range of needs.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'Which of these is a financial method of motivating employees?', options: ['Commission and bonuses', 'Job rotation between tasks', 'Job enrichment', 'Autonomy at work'], correct: 0, explain: 'Commission and bonuses are financial rewards linked to sales performance or achieving targets, motivating employees through extra pay.', misconception: 'Job rotation, job enrichment and autonomy are non-financial methods of motivation, focused on the nature of the work itself rather than pay.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'Which of these is a non-financial method of motivating employees?', options: ['Job enrichment: more challenging, varied tasks', 'A permanent pay rise for all members of staff', 'A bonus for hitting the team’s sales targets', 'Commission paid on each sale that is made'], correct: 0, explain: 'Job enrichment is a non-financial method of motivation that involves making a job more interesting and rewarding by adding responsibility or variety.', misconception: 'Pay rises, bonuses and commission are all financial rewards, distinct from non-financial methods like job enrichment or autonomy.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'What does job rotation involve?', options: ['Moving staff between tasks to cut boredom and widen skills', 'Giving an employee a permanent pay rise to reward good work', 'Removing an employee’s responsibilities entirely to reduce stress', 'Reducing the number of tasks an employee performs to just one'], correct: 0, explain: 'Job rotation involves moving employees between different tasks or roles, which can reduce monotony and help them develop a broader skill set.', misconception: 'Job rotation is about varying tasks, not about pay changes or narrowing an employee’s responsibilities to a single task.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'What is meant by autonomy as a method of motivation?', options: ['More freedom and responsibility to make decisions about their work', 'Paying employees a fixed hourly wage, however much they produce', 'Closely supervising every task that an employee completes each day', 'Reducing an employee’s decision-making power to avoid any mistakes'], correct: 0, explain: 'Autonomy means giving employees more control and freedom over how they carry out their work, which can increase motivation and job satisfaction.', misconception: 'Autonomy is about increasing freedom and trust, not close supervision or reducing an employee’s decision-making power.', tag: 'Motivation' },
    { q: 'Which best describes an organisation with a flat structure?', options: ['Few levels of hierarchy and a wide span of control', 'Many levels of hierarchy and a narrow span of control', 'No employees other than the owner', 'A structure used only by public limited companies'], correct: 0, explain: 'A flat organisational structure has relatively few levels of hierarchy, meaning managers typically oversee a wider span of control.', misconception: 'A structure with many levels and narrow spans of control describes a hierarchical (tall) structure, the opposite of flat.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What does “span of control” refer to?', options: ['The number of people a manager directly supervises', 'The number of levels in the organisation’s hierarchy', 'The total number of employees in the whole business', 'The geographical area in which the business operates'], correct: 0, explain: 'Span of control refers to the number of employees or subordinates that a single manager is directly responsible for supervising.', misconception: 'This is different from the number of hierarchy levels, which refers to the organisation’s overall structure, not one manager’s direct reports.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What does “chain of command” refer to?', options: ['The line of authority from senior managers down to employees', 'The number of employees that a manager supervises directly', 'The total number of departments in the whole business', 'The process of recruiting and selecting new members of staff'], correct: 0, explain: 'Chain of command describes the formal path of authority and communication from the top of an organisation down through each level of management to employees.', misconception: 'Chain of command is about the line of authority, not the number of staff one manager supervises, which is span of control.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What is delegation?', options: ['Passing authority for a task down to a subordinate', 'A manager completing every task personally to keep control', 'The formal process of dismissing an employee from the business', 'A method of financial motivation, such as profit sharing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Delegation involves a manager giving a subordinate the authority and responsibility to carry out a specific task or decision.', misconception: 'Delegation is about sharing tasks and authority, not about a manager doing everything alone, dismissing staff, or financial reward.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What is a possible drawback of centralised decision-making?', options: ['Decisions can be slower and may ignore local knowledge', 'It always leads to faster decision-making at every level of the business', 'It guarantees higher employee motivation across all of the departments', 'It removes the need for any senior managers at the business’s head office'], correct: 0, explain: 'Centralisation keeps decision-making power at the top of the organisation, which can slow decisions and mean senior managers lack local knowledge that lower-level staff might have.', misconception: 'Centralisation typically slows, rather than speeds up, decisions made at lower levels, since approval must come from the centre.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What is a possible benefit of decentralisation?', options: ['Decisions can be made faster by people with better local knowledge', 'It guarantees consistent decision-making across the whole of the business', 'It removes the need for any communication between different departments', 'It always reduces the business’s costs by cutting the number of managers'], correct: 0, explain: 'Decentralisation spreads decision-making authority to lower levels, allowing faster decisions by people with relevant local knowledge and experience.', misconception: 'Decentralisation can actually make consistency across the business harder to achieve, since different parts may make different decisions.', tag: 'Organisation' },
    { q: 'What is the main role of the human resources (HR) function?', options: ['Workforce planning, recruitment, training and welfare', 'Managing the business’s cash flow and its budgets', 'Designing and manufacturing the business’s products', 'Carrying out market research on customer needs'], correct: 0, explain: 'Human resources is responsible for workforce planning, recruitment and selection, training, and supporting staff welfare and employment issues.', misconception: 'Cash flow and budgets are managed by the finance function, and product design and manufacturing fall under production, not HR.', tag: 'Functional areas' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is a responsibility of the finance function?', options: ['Wages, cash-flow forecasts, budgets and accounts', 'Market research and planning new products', 'Recruiting and training new members of staff', 'Manufacturing the product and controlling quality'], correct: 0, explain: 'The finance function manages the business’s money, including paying wages and salaries, cash-flow forecasting, budgeting and accounting.', misconception: 'Market research and product planning are marketing responsibilities, while recruitment and training are HR responsibilities, not finance.', tag: 'Functional areas' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is a responsibility of the marketing function?', options: ['Market research, pricing, promotion and advertising', 'Cash-flow forecasting and setting departmental budgets', 'Recruitment, selection and training of new staff', 'Quality control of finished products before sale'], correct: 0, explain: 'Marketing is responsible for researching customer needs, and deciding on pricing, promotion and advertising to sell the product effectively.', misconception: 'Cash flow and budgeting belong to finance, while quality control is typically a production responsibility, not marketing.', tag: 'Functional areas' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is a responsibility of the production function?', options: ['Making and designing products, and controlling quality and stock', 'Handling employment issues and staff grievances at work', 'Managing the business’s wages, salaries and pension payments', 'Carrying out sales promotions and advertising campaigns'], correct: 0, explain: 'The production function is responsible for actually manufacturing the product, along with product design, quality control and stock control.', misconception: 'Staff grievances are handled by HR, wages by finance, and promotion and advertising by marketing — not production.', tag: 'Functional areas' },
    { q: 'Why is it important for functional areas of a business to communicate with each other?', options: ['Decisions in one area, such as a new product, affect others such as production', 'Each functional area operates completely independently, with no effect on others', 'Only the human resources department needs to communicate with other departments', 'It is a legal requirement for all businesses to hold weekly meetings between departments'], correct: 0, explain: 'Functional areas are interdependent — for example, a marketing decision to launch a new product affects what production must make and how finance must budget — so communication between them is essential.', misconception: 'Functional areas are not independent of each other; decisions in one area regularly have knock-on effects for the others.', tag: 'Functional areas' },
    { q: 'How does human resources support a business during dismissal or redundancy?', options: ['By making sure the process follows employment law and is fair', 'By deciding the business’s pricing strategy for its products', 'By manufacturing replacement products for unhappy customers', 'By setting the business’s cash-flow forecast for the year ahead'], correct: 0, explain: 'HR manages employment issues such as disciplinary procedures, dismissal, unfair dismissal and redundancy, ensuring the business follows relevant employment law.', misconception: 'Pricing, manufacturing and cash-flow forecasting are the responsibility of marketing, production and finance respectively, not HR.', tag: 'Functional areas' }
  ];

