  var BUS5_QUESTION_BANK = [
    { q: 'What are economies of scale?', options: ['Cost advantages from growing in scale', 'A rise in average costs as a business grows larger', 'A type of promotional pricing strategy used at launch', 'The total revenue earned by a business in one year'], correct: 0, explain: 'Economies of scale are the cost savings a business achieves as output increases, spreading fixed costs over more units and often gaining bulk-buying discounts, lowering average cost per unit.', misconception: 'Rising average costs as a business grows too large describes diseconomies of scale, the opposite effect.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'Which of these is an example of an internal economy of scale?', options: ['Bulk-buying raw materials at a discount for larger orders', 'A new motorway built near the business by the government', 'A general fall in national interest rates on loans', 'A change in exchange rates that makes imports cheaper'], correct: 0, explain: 'Bulk-buying discounts are an internal economy of scale, arising directly from the business’s own growth in purchasing power.', misconception: 'A new motorway, interest rate changes and exchange rate changes are external factors outside the business’s control, not internal economies.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What is an external economy of scale?', options: ['An advantage from the growth of the whole industry or area', 'A cost advantage gained only through a business’s own bulk buying', 'A type of diseconomy caused by a business becoming too large', 'A pricing strategy used when launching a new product'], correct: 0, explain: 'External economies of scale arise from factors outside an individual business, such as improved local infrastructure or a skilled local workforce, benefiting all businesses in that industry or area.', misconception: 'Economies arising purely from one business’s own bulk-buying power are internal, not external, economies of scale.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What are diseconomies of scale?', options: ['Rising average costs when a business grows too large', 'Falling average costs per unit as output increases', 'A method of quality control used in large factories', 'A source of finance for large businesses that grow'], correct: 0, explain: 'Diseconomies of scale occur when a business becomes too large to manage efficiently, causing average costs per unit to rise, for example due to poor communication or coordination problems.', misconception: 'Falling average costs as output increases describes economies of scale, the opposite of diseconomies.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'Why might a business experience diseconomies of scale as it grows very large?', options: ['Communication and coordination get harder, causing inefficiency', 'It will always benefit from unlimited bulk-buying discounts', 'Its fixed costs are automatically eliminated as it grows', 'It becomes legally required to reduce its level of output'], correct: 0, explain: 'As a business grows very large, it can become harder to manage and communicate effectively across the whole organisation, leading to inefficiencies and rising average costs.', misconception: 'Bulk-buying discounts do not increase without limit, and there is no legal requirement forcing a large business to cut output.', tag: 'Economies of scale' },
    { q: 'What best describes job production?', options: ['Making a single, often unique, item to a customer’s requirements', 'Producing large volumes of an identical, standardised product continuously', 'Producing items in identifiable groups or batches, one after another', 'Producing goods using machines only, with no labour at all'], correct: 0, explain: 'Job production involves making one unique product at a time, often tailored to a specific customer, such as a bespoke wedding cake or a custom-built ship.', misconception: 'Producing large volumes of an identical product continuously describes flow production, not job production.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key characteristic of batch production?', options: ['Producing goods in groups, each batch possibly slightly different', 'Producing only one unique item at a time for a single customer', 'Producing a continuous stream of an identical product with no breaks', 'Producing goods without following any set process or plan'], correct: 0, explain: 'Batch production involves making a set quantity (a batch) of a product before switching the process to make a different batch, allowing some variation between batches.', misconception: 'Continuous, identical output with no breaks describes flow production, while single unique items describe job production, not batch.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key characteristic of flow production?', options: ['Continuous, high-volume production of identical products', 'Making a single, unique product to order for one particular customer', 'Making small groups of similar products at a time, then switching', 'Producing goods only when a specific customer has placed an order'], correct: 0, explain: 'Flow production involves a continuous process, often on an assembly line, producing large volumes of a standardised, identical product efficiently.', misconception: 'Producing to order for a single customer describes job production, distinct from the continuous, mass nature of flow production.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a key advantage of flow production?', options: ['Low unit costs through economies of scale at high volumes', 'It allows every product to be fully customised for each customer', 'It requires very little upfront investment in machinery or equipment', 'It is the most flexible method when demand changes frequently'], correct: 0, explain: 'Flow production can achieve low costs per unit due to the large volumes produced, benefiting from economies of scale, though it requires significant investment and is relatively inflexible.', misconception: 'Flow production is generally less flexible and requires high upfront investment, and it does not offer full product customisation.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is the difference between labour-intensive and capital-intensive production?', options: ['Labour-intensive relies mainly on workers; capital-intensive on machinery', 'Labour-intensive production always costs more than capital-intensive production', 'Capital-intensive production requires no initial investment in equipment', 'There is no meaningful difference between the two methods of production'], correct: 0, explain: 'Labour-intensive production depends primarily on human workers to carry out tasks, while capital-intensive production relies mainly on machinery, technology and equipment.', misconception: 'Capital-intensive production usually requires significant upfront investment in machinery, contrary to requiring none.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'A factory produces 4,800 units in a week using 40 workers, each working 40 hours. What is labour productivity, in units per worker?', options: ['120 units per worker', '4,800 units per worker', '40 units per worker', '192,000 units per worker'], correct: 0, explain: 'Labour productivity (units per worker) = total output ÷ number of workers = 4,800 ÷ 40 = 120 units per worker.', misconception: 'A common error is dividing by total hours worked instead of the number of workers, or forgetting to divide at all.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'How can improvements in productivity benefit a business?', options: ['They lower the cost of each unit, improving competitiveness', 'They always require a business to reduce its output', 'They guarantee an increase in the number of employees needed', 'They have no effect at all on a business’s costs or prices'], correct: 0, explain: 'Higher productivity means more output is produced for the same input (such as labour or time), which can lower unit costs and improve a business’s competitiveness and profitability.', misconception: 'Improved productivity does not require reducing output — quite the opposite, it usually means producing the same or more with the same or fewer resources.', tag: 'Production processes' },
    { q: 'What is a core principle of lean production?', options: ['Minimising waste of all kinds to cut costs', 'Producing as much stock as possible in advance, whatever the demand', 'Ignoring product quality in favour of speed of production', 'Avoiding any form of continuous improvement in the factory'], correct: 0, explain: 'Lean production focuses on eliminating waste — of time, materials, or effort — throughout the production process, improving efficiency and reducing costs.', misconception: 'Lean production actually aims to reduce excess stock and waste, and it typically goes hand-in-hand with maintaining or improving quality.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'How does just-in-time (JIT) production operate?', options: ['Materials arrive only as they are needed, so little stock is held', 'Large amounts of stock are ordered well in advance and stored for months', 'Products are made in advance without following any customer orders', 'All raw materials are bought once a year in a single large order'], correct: 0, explain: 'JIT production involves ordering and receiving materials only when needed for production, reducing the amount of stock a business needs to hold and store.', misconception: 'Storing large amounts of stock in advance is the opposite of JIT, which is often contrasted with traditional stockpiling approaches.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'What is a key risk of using just-in-time (JIT) production?', options: ['A supply disruption can quickly stop production, as little stock is held', 'It always increases the amount of stock a business must store in warehouses', 'It guarantees that a business will never run out of materials or components', 'It removes the need for reliable suppliers who deliver on time'], correct: 0, explain: 'Because JIT relies on minimal stock levels, any delay or disruption in the supply chain can quickly bring production to a halt, since there is little buffer stock to fall back on.', misconception: 'JIT reduces stored stock rather than increasing it, and it actually depends heavily on having reliable, well-coordinated suppliers.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'What is the principle behind Kaizen (continuous improvement)?', options: ['Small, ongoing improvements, often involving all employees', 'Making one very large change to production once every ten years', 'Focusing only on senior managers’ decisions, without employee input', 'Avoiding any changes to existing working methods in the factory'], correct: 0, explain: 'Kaizen involves making small, continuous improvements to processes over time, often drawing on the ideas and involvement of employees at all levels of the business.', misconception: 'Kaizen is about frequent, incremental change involving staff input, not a single large change or a management-only approach.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'Why is using resources effectively important to a business?', options: ['It can cut costs and waste, improving efficiency', 'It has no effect at all on a business’s profitability', 'It only matters for businesses in the manufacturing sector', 'It always requires a business to increase its workforce'], correct: 0, explain: 'Using resources — such as materials, time and labour — effectively helps reduce waste and cost, supporting greater efficiency and competitiveness across any type of business.', misconception: 'Effective resource use matters across all sectors, not just manufacturing, and it is about efficiency, not necessarily hiring more staff.', tag: 'Lean production' },
    { q: 'How can robotics be used in production?', options: ['To automate repetitive tasks with more speed and precision', 'Only to complete tasks that require creative human judgement', 'To completely replace the need for any product design', 'Only in the service sector, never in manufacturing'], correct: 0, explain: 'Robotics can automate repetitive or precise manufacturing tasks, improving consistency, speed and accuracy in the production process.', misconception: 'Robotics is most suited to repetitive, well-defined tasks rather than tasks needing creative judgement, and it is widely used in manufacturing.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'What is a limitation of using robotics in production?', options: ['High upfront costs and a possible need to retrain or cut staff', 'Robots always produce lower-quality output than human workers', 'Robots cannot be used safely in any kind of factory setting', 'Robotics removes the need for any maintenance or repairs'], correct: 0, explain: 'Introducing robotics can require significant initial investment, and may lead to job losses or a need to retrain staff to work alongside new technology.', misconception: 'Robotics is often used specifically to improve consistency and precision, so it does not generally lower output quality compared with manual production.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'How can new production technology increase flexibility for a business?', options: ['It lets production be reprogrammed more easily for different products', 'It always makes a business less able to respond to changes in demand', 'It removes the need for any human oversight of the production process', 'It only benefits businesses that use flow production on assembly lines'], correct: 0, explain: 'Modern production technology can often be reprogrammed or adjusted relatively easily, allowing a business to switch between products or respond to changing customer demand.', misconception: 'New technology tends to improve, not reduce, a business’s ability to respond to changing demand, and human oversight is still typically needed.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'What should a business consider when assessing the impact of introducing new production technology?', options: ['The effect on costs, quality, staff and retraining needs', 'Only the initial purchase price of the new technology', 'Only how the technology looks compared with competitors’ equipment', 'Nothing, as new technology always benefits a business in every way'], correct: 0, explain: 'Assessing new technology involves weighing up its effect on costs, product quality, and its impact on employees, including potential job losses or the need for retraining.', misconception: 'New technology brings both potential benefits and risks — it does not automatically benefit a business in every respect.', tag: 'Technology in production' },
    { q: 'Which of these is one of the four factors of production?', options: ['Land', 'Advertising', 'Market share', 'Break-even'], correct: 0, explain: 'The four factors of production are enterprise, capital, land and labour — the resources needed to produce goods and services.', misconception: 'Advertising, market share and break-even are marketing or finance concepts, not among the four factors of production.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What is the role of “enterprise” as a factor of production?', options: ['Taking the risk of organising the other factors of production', 'Providing the raw materials used in the production process', 'Providing the physical effort of workers in the business', 'Providing the money invested in machinery and buildings'], correct: 0, explain: 'Enterprise refers to the entrepreneurial skill of organising the other factors of production — land, labour and capital — and taking on the associated business risk.', misconception: 'Providing raw materials relates to land, providing physical effort relates to labour, and providing machinery relates to capital, not enterprise.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “capital” refer to as a factor of production?', options: ['Man-made resources such as machinery, tools and buildings', 'Natural resources such as raw materials and the land itself', 'The workforce employed by a business to make its products', 'The risk-taking involved in starting and running a business'], correct: 0, explain: 'Capital, as a factor of production, refers to man-made resources such as machinery, equipment, tools and buildings used to produce goods and services.', misconception: 'Natural resources are the “land” factor of production, and the workforce is “labour” — distinct from capital.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “land” refer to as a factor of production?', options: ['Natural resources, including land and raw materials', 'The buildings and machinery that a business owns', 'The workers employed to carry out tasks in the business', 'The money used to fund the business’s operations'], correct: 0, explain: 'Land, as a factor of production, covers natural resources, including the physical land itself and raw materials such as minerals, timber and water.', misconception: 'Buildings and machinery are capital, and workers are labour — both distinct from the natural-resource factor of land.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'Why might a business choose a labour-intensive method of production?', options: ['Where tasks need flexibility or judgement, or labour is cheap', 'Because labour-intensive methods always produce output faster than machines', 'Because it removes the need for a business to pay any wages', 'Because labour-intensive production always has lower total costs'], correct: 0, explain: 'A business may choose labour-intensive production where flexibility or human skill and judgement are valuable, or where labour costs are relatively low compared with investing in machinery.', misconception: 'Labour-intensive production still requires paying wages, and it is not automatically faster or cheaper than capital-intensive methods — it depends on the specific business context.', tag: 'Factors of production' },
    { q: 'What does “quality” mean in relation to goods and services?', options: ['Meeting or exceeding customer expectations of reliability and standard', 'Producing the cheapest possible version of a product for customers', 'Producing the largest possible quantity of a product each day', 'Using the newest available production technology, whatever the product'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality refers to how well a good or service meets customer expectations and its intended purpose, including factors such as reliability, durability and standard of finish.', misconception: 'Quality is about meeting customer expectations, not simply about being the cheapest, the most abundant, or made with the newest technology.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'How can high quality benefit a business?', options: ['It can improve satisfaction and reputation, giving a competitive edge', 'It always increases production costs, with no other benefit to the business', 'It removes the need for any quality checks in the future', 'It has no impact on customer loyalty or on repeat purchases'], correct: 0, explain: 'High-quality goods and services can boost customer satisfaction and loyalty, strengthen a business’s reputation, and help it stand out from competitors.', misconception: 'While maintaining quality can involve costs, it typically brings offsetting benefits like stronger reputation and customer loyalty, not cost with no return.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is the main principle of quality control?', options: ['Checking products for faults, usually at the end of production', 'Building quality checks into every stage, carried out by every employee', 'Ignoring product quality until a customer makes a complaint', 'Reducing production speed to zero to guarantee that there are no faults'], correct: 0, explain: 'Quality control typically involves inspecting products, often at the end of production, to identify and remove faulty items before they reach customers.', misconception: 'Involving every employee in checking quality at every stage more closely describes Total Quality Management (TQM), rather than traditional quality control.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is a limitation of quality control compared with Total Quality Management (TQM)?', options: ['Faults and waste may already have happened before the end checks', 'Quality control involves every employee in checking quality throughout production', 'Quality control guarantees that there will be zero product defects', 'Quality control is always more expensive to introduce than TQM'], correct: 0, explain: 'Because quality control often checks products only at the end of the process, faults may have already led to wasted materials and time earlier in production — a key limitation compared with TQM’s continuous approach.', misconception: 'It is TQM, not traditional quality control, that typically involves all employees checking quality throughout the whole production process.', tag: 'Quality' },
    { q: 'What is the core idea behind Total Quality Management (TQM)?', options: ['Every employee, at every stage, takes responsibility for quality', 'Only a single quality inspector checks products at the very end of production', 'Quality is only the responsibility of senior management and directors', 'Quality checks should only happen once a year during an audit'], correct: 0, explain: 'TQM is an approach where quality is everyone’s responsibility, built into every stage of production, aiming to prevent faults rather than just detect them at the end.', misconception: 'A single end-of-line inspector describes basic quality control, not the whole-business, prevention-focused approach of TQM.', tag: 'Quality' }
  ];

